From f87d3ca160f04bc58c7dbac1f43c031a7082a4c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Redgrave961 <214504937+Redgrave961@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 02:18:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] SPU GETLLAR snapshots are atomic; texture eviction; Linux
 write tracking

GETLLAR's lock-free fast path copied the 128-byte line without checking for
a writer, so a PPU store landing mid-copy gave the SPURS kernel a torn
control block and it halted on its own consistency assert (the gameplay
freeze). The line lock now carries a sequence count (odd while a writer is
inside); the fast path copies, revalidates, and recopies under the lock if a
writer overlapped. New stress test test_spu_getllar_snapshot reproduces the
tear (thousands per run before, none after).

Textures unused for 1800 flips are evicted every 256 flips through the
deferred-destroy list. Texture change detection is one path for both
platforms with a per-platform page scan: GetWriteWatch on Windows,
asynchronous userfaultfd write-protection with PAGEMAP_SCAN on Linux (which
also sees writes made by system calls). Linux guest memory is a page-aligned
MAP_NORESERVE reservation like the Windows reserve.
